      {
        "id": "p38-q8-10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $A$ and $B$ are independent, then $A$ and $\\overline{B}$ are independent.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Since $A$ and $B$ are independent,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(A\\cap\\overline{B})&=P(A)-P(A\\cap B)\\\\P(A\\cap\\overline{B})&=P(A)-P(A)P(B)\\\\P(A\\cap\\overline{B})&=P(A)(1-P(B))\\\\P(A\\cap\\overline{B})&=P(A)P(\\overline{B}).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $A$ and $\\overline{B}$ are independent. The statement is true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-q8-10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $A$ and $B$ are independent, then $\\overline{A}$ and $B$ are independent.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Since $A$ and $B$ are independent,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\overline{A}\\cap B)&=P(B)-P(A\\cap B)\\\\P(\\overline{A}\\cap B)&=P(B)-P(A)P(B)\\\\P(\\overline{A}\\cap B)&=(1-P(A))P(B)\\\\P(\\overline{A}\\cap B)&=P(\\overline{A})P(B).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $\\overline{A}$ and $B$ are independent. The statement is true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-q8-10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If $A$, $B$ are independent, and $B$, $C$ are also independent, then $A$, $C$ must be independent.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "For a counterexample, roll a fair die and flip a fair coin independently. Let $A$ be a die result greater than $3$, $B$ be heads and $C$ be a die result less than $5$. Both $A$ and $B$, and $B$ and $C$, are independent. However,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(A\\cap C)&=\\frac{1}{6}\\\\P(A)P(C)&=\\frac{3}{6}\\times\\frac{4}{6}\\\\P(A)P(C)&=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $P(A\\cap C)\\ne P(A)P(C)$, $A$ and $C$ are dependent. The statement is false.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
